    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps sit at 4.8% of total volume and only 2 sets, the lowest of any group tracked. Biceps do receive indirect stimulus from back training, but consistent direct work is still valuable for balanced arm development, especially given the goal of building muscle."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": false,
  "deloadReason": null,
  "actionItems": [
    "Prioritize completing all four planned sessions next week — Upper A, Upper B, Lower A, and Lower B. Full routine adherence is the single most impactful change you can make right now, as missing half your sessions this week is the clearest reason strength indexes stalled.",
    "On your next Lower session, aim to add a small amount of load or an extra rep to your main leg movements. The legs index dipped from 106 to 105, so giving legs a deliberate nudge back upward is a useful short-term target.",
    "Add one additional direct set for shoulders and one for biceps to address the mild volume gap these groups showed this period. Even a single extra set per group across the week can meaningfully improve balance over time.",
    "Review what caused you to miss Upper B and Lower B this week — whether it was scheduling, fatigue, or something else — and put a simple plan in place to protect those sessions next week, since consistency over time is the foundation of muscle growth."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "Half the planned sessions were missed this period, causing strength indexes to stall across all muscle groups; restoring full four-session adherence is the top priority for next period."
}
